utlives the pointer this"},{"DocComment":" function returns, or else it will end up dangling."},{"DocComment":" Modifying the vector may cause its buffer to be reallocated,"},{"DocComment":" which would also make any pointers to it invalid."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" This method guarantees that for the purpose of the aliasing model, this method"},{"DocComment":" does not materialize a reference to the underlying slice, and thus the returned pointer"},{"DocComment":" will remain valid when mixed with other calls to [`as_ptr`], [`as_mut_ptr`],"},{"DocComment":" and [`as_non_null`]."},{"DocComment":" Note that calling other methods that materialize references to the slice,"},{"DocComment":" or references to specific elements you are planning on accessing through this pointer,"},{"DocComment":" may still invalidate this pointer."},{"DocComment":" See the second example below for how this guarantee can be used."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" The method also guarantees that, as long as `T` is not zero-sized and the capacity is"},{"DocComment":" nonzero, the pointer may be passed into [`dealloc`] with a layout of"},{"DocComment":" `Layout::array::<T>(capacity)` in order to deallocate the backing memory. If this is done,"},{"DocComment":" be careful not to run the destructor of the `Vec`, as dropping it will result in"},{"DocComment":" double-frees. Wrapping the `Vec` in a [`ManuallyDrop`] is the typical way to achieve this."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # Examples"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" // Allocate vector big enough for 4 elements."},{"DocComment":" let size = 4;"},{"DocComment":" let mut x: Vec<i32> = Vec::with_capacity(size);"},{"DocComment":" let x_ptr = x.as_mut_ptr();"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" // Initialize elements via raw pointer writes, then set length."},{"DocComment":" unsafe {"},{"DocComment":"     for i in 0..size {"},{"DocComment":"         *x_ptr.add(i) = i as i32;"},{"DocComment":"     }"},{"Do