butes":[{"DocComment":" Raw transmutation from `u64`."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" This is currently identical to `transmute::<u64, f64>(v)` on all platforms."},{"DocComment":" It turns out this is incredibly portable, for two reasons:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" * Floats and Ints have the same endianness on all supported platforms."},{"DocComment":" * IEEE 754 very precisely specifies the bit layout of floats."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" However there is one caveat: prior to the 2008 version of IEEE 754, how"},{"DocComment":" to interpret the NaN signaling bit wasn't actually specified. Most platforms"},{"DocComment":" (notably x86 and ARM) picked the interpretation that was ultimately"},{"DocComment":" standardized in 2008, but some didn't (notably MIPS). As a result, all"},{"DocComment":" signaling NaNs on MIPS are quiet NaNs on x86, and vice-versa."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Rather than trying to preserve signaling-ness cross-platform, this"},{"DocComment":" implementation favors preserving the exact bits. This means that"},{"DocComment":" any payloads encoded in NaNs will be preserved even if the result of"},{"DocComment":" this method is sent over the network from an x86 machine to a MIPS one."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" If the results of this method are only manipulated by the same"},{"DocComment":" architecture that produced them, then there is no portability concern."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" If the input isn't NaN, then there is no portability concern."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" If you don't care about signaling-ness (very likely), then there is no"},{"DocComment":" portability concern."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Note that this function is distinct from `as` casting, which attempts to"},{"DocComment":" preserve the *numeric* value, and not the bitwise value."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # Examples"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" let v = f64::from_bits(0x402